## Task 3 How Splunk Processes Data

Splunk turns raw logs into structured events by identifying formats, assigning sourcetypes, breaking events, and extracting fields.
- You control ingestion with inputs.conf, parsing rules with props.conf, and optional field extractions.
- Default configs live in /opt/splunk/etc/system/default, while custom overrides go in /opt/splunk/etc/system/local.
- Splunk’s UI can also manage sourcetypes, field extractions, and parsing settings without editing files manually.


Splunk turns raw logs into structured events by identifying timestamps, breaking events, assigning sourcetypes, and extracting fields. You control ingestion with inputs.conf, parsing behavior with props.conf, and optional field extractions.

1. Ingesting Logs (inputs.conf)
You tell Splunk which log file to monitor and how to classify it:
```
[monitor:///path/to/your/data]
disabled = false
index = your_index
sourcetype = your_sourcetype
```
This defines the log source, index, and sourcetype — the foundation for all later parsing.

2. Parsing Log Structure (props.conf)
You define how Splunk should interpret the log format:
```
[your_sourcetype]
SHOULD_LINEMERGE = false
TIME_FORMAT = %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S
```
This controls event breaking, multi‑line handling, and timestamp recognition.

3. Extracting Fields from Logs
You can pull values out of log lines using regex‑based field extractions:
```
[your_sourcetype]
EXTRACT-fieldname1 = regular_expression1
EXTRACT-fieldname2 = regular_expression2
```
These turn raw log content into searchable fields.

4. UI Alternative
Splunk’s web interface can configure sourcetypes, field extractions, and parsing rules without editing files manually.

